Starting with the original equation:
\begin{align*}
|n + 6| &= 2 - n \\
\end{align*}

Since the absolute value function always yields a non-negative result, we have:
\begin{align*}
n + 6 &= 2 - n \\
\end{align*}

To solve for $n$, we add $n$ to both sides of the equation and then subtract 2 from both sides:
\begin{align*}
n + n + 6 &= 2 - n + n - 2 \\
2n + 6 &= -2 \\
2n &= -2 - 6 \\
2n &= -8 \\
n &= \frac{-8}{2}\\
n &= \boxed{-4}\\
\end{align*}

