The area of a triangle with vertices at $(x_1, y_1)$, $(x_2, y_2)$, and $(x_3, y_3)$ can be found using the formula:
\begin{align*}
\text{Area} &= \frac{1}{2} \left| x_1(y_2 - y_3) + x_2(y_3 - y_1) + x_3(y_1 - y_2) \right|
\end{align*}
Plugging in the coordinates $(0,0)$, $(0,5)$, and $(7,12)$, we get:
\begin{align*}
\text{Area} &= \frac{1}{2} \left| 0(5 - 12) + 0(12 - 0) + 7(0 - 5) \right| \\
&= \frac{1}{2} \left| -35 \right| \\
&= \frac{1}{2} \cdot 35 \\
&= \boxed{17.5}
\end{align*}
The answer is: 17.5

\begin{align*}
\binom{6}{2}x^2y^4 &= \boxed{15}x^2y^4 \text{ terms}\end{align*}.
The coefficient of the $x^2y^4$ term in the expansion of $(x+y)^6$ is given by the binomial coefficient $\binom{6}{2}$. The binomial coefficient $\binom{6}{2}$ represents the number of ways to choose 2 $x^2$ terms out of 6, which is calculated as follows:
\begin{align*}
\binom{6}{2} &= \frac{6!}{2!(6-2)!} \\
&= \frac{6!}{2!4!} \\
&= \frac{6 \times 5}{2 \times 1} \quad \text{(simplifying the factorials)} \\
&= 15 \quad \text{(the value of the binomial coefficient)}.
\end{align*}
Therefore, the coefficient of the $x^2y^4$ term in the expansion of $(x+y)^6$ is $\boxed{15}$.

Let the cost of a pen be $p$ cents and the cost of a pencil be $q$ cents.
From the given information, we can set up the following system of equations:
\begin{align*}
4p + 3q &= 224 \quad \text{(1)} \\
2p + 5q &= 154 \quad \text{(2)}
\end{align*}
Multiplying equation (2) by 2, we get:
\begin{align*}
4p + 10q &= 308 \quad \text{(3)}
\end{align*}
Subtracting equation (1) from equation (3), we get:
\begin{align*}
7q &= 84 \\
q &= 12
\end{align*}
Therefore, the cost of a pencil is $\boxed{12}$ cents. The answer is: 12

